  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "Can I reimburse myself out of my HSA?",
      "answer": "Yes, you can reimburse yourself out of your HSA for qualified medical expenses incurred after the account was established under state law. You must maintain records showing the expenses were not reimbursed by insurance or other sources and were not claimed as an itemized deduction on your tax return."
    },
    {
      "question": "Does HSA reimbursement require a receipt?",
      "answer": "IRS rules require sufficient records showing that your HSA distribution paid or reimbursed qualified medical expenses. Keep itemized receipts and other records showing the expense was not paid elsewhere or claimed as an itemized deduction. Keep these with your tax records rather than sending them with your return."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can an FSA reimburse an expense before the service happens?",
      "answer": "No, an FSA cannot advance reimburse future or projected expenses. Under IRS Publication 969, expenses are only eligible for FSA reimbursement after the medical care has actually been provided during your active coverage period."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I reimburse a medical membership fee?",
      "answer": "A medical membership fee can only be reimbursed if it meets specific federal tax criteria. Fees for general health improvement or arrangements paid by employer contributions do not qualify."
    }
  ],
